WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.280
Narrator: Commercial kitchens
from our favorite restaurants

00:00:02.280 --> 00:00:03.563
and fast-food places

00:00:03.563 --> 00:00:06.600
produce some of the toughest
and thickest grease.

00:00:06.600 --> 00:00:08.010
If left uncleaned,

00:00:08.010 --> 00:00:10.863
it can cause serious
damage to sewage systems,

00:00:10.863 --> 00:00:13.313
health risks from contaminated food,

00:00:13.313 --> 00:00:15.146
and even kitchen fires.

00:00:16.246 --> 00:00:18.570
Here's how some of the greasiest parts

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:21.778
of restaurant kitchens are
professionally deep cleaned.

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.881
Jay Lopez, the owner of Cleannex,

00:00:25.881 --> 00:00:27.855
walks us through how his team

00:00:27.855 --> 00:00:30.038
cleans a restaurant's
kitchen exhaust system,

00:00:30.038 --> 00:00:33.107
which extracts the fumes from the kitchen.

00:00:33.107 --> 00:00:35.230
Jay: Today, I'm going to
be walking you through

00:00:35.230 --> 00:00:38.739
how we clean the kitchen
exhaust system in a restaurant.

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:42.150
The purpose of a kitchen
exhaust system is to

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:47.010
extract the fumes from a
kitchen in a commercial setting.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:50.070
In order to remove grease,
you have to understand grease.

00:00:50.070 --> 00:00:52.920
What holds the oil together is the fat,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:55.950
and once the first layer is up,

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.300
it becomes a magnet,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.875
and it attracts more and more and more.

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:04.770
Everything is cleaned from top to bottom,

00:01:04.770 --> 00:01:07.290
and you will start cleaning the fan first.

00:01:07.290 --> 00:01:09.208
We're using a high-end degreaser

00:01:09.208 --> 00:01:13.110
with a little potassium
hydroxide and sodium hydroxide.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:16.260
Those two chemicals
combined create miracles

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.240
in destroying the fat and the grease.

00:01:18.240 --> 00:01:22.156
In our case, we time it somewhere
between 10 to 15 minutes.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.737
Once we see that the
grease starts to melt,

00:01:26.737 --> 00:01:30.803
at that point we know we
need to apply hot water

00:01:30.803 --> 00:01:33.245
and remove it, because
anything longer at that point

00:01:33.245 --> 00:01:35.212
is going to start to destroy the metal.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.070
Once the fan has been cleaned,

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:39.660
the next step would be the duct.

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:42.843
That's the part that
connects the fan to the hood.

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.020
This level is considered extreme.

00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.600
That was a fire waiting to happen.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.510
I would say this is about
a year worth of grease.

00:01:51.510 --> 00:01:53.850
It all depends on the volume of cooking.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:56.370
If this is moderate cooking,

00:01:56.370 --> 00:01:58.560
this is about a year's worth of grease.

00:01:58.560 --> 00:01:59.710
That's a lot.

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:05.550
The duct is cleaned in
a few different steps.

00:02:05.550 --> 00:02:08.970
During this process, we use
a tool made by Guardian.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.960
This tool is known as the
EZ magnetic duct scraper.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.240
It has magnets set into the tool

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.441
and a razor blade on each side.

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:20.850
Once it's clenched to the duct,

00:02:20.850 --> 00:02:23.783
it is manually moved forward and backwards

00:02:23.783 --> 00:02:25.800
until the grease is removed.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:27.982
This forward-and-backward method assures

00:02:27.982 --> 00:02:32.070
that the grease buildup will
be removed from both sides.

00:02:32.070 --> 00:02:36.870
It'll go from the yellow color
all the way into the black.

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:40.620
When it's in the black level,
that's an extreme level.

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:45.512
That means that the system has
not been properly maintained.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:48.480
In these types of designs,

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.450
we'll use duct spinners and duct brushes.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:54.600
The duct spinner is
designed to self-rotate

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:58.710
within a protective cage and
spray water at high speed,

00:02:58.710 --> 00:03:01.953
which dislodge buildup
from the metal duct.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:08.280
We use a combination of a
foaming agent and a degreaser.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.950
The foam is used as a delivering agent,

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.690
and it allows the degreaser to stay

00:03:12.690 --> 00:03:16.050
in contact with the grease
for a longer period.

00:03:16.050 --> 00:03:17.760
If foam was not used,

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.498
the degreaser would drip off the grease.

00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:27.390
The next step would be
the hood and the filters.

00:03:27.390 --> 00:03:30.150
See, the filters are your
first line of defense.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:33.780
What the filters is doing
is holding back heat,

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.858
and it's holding back most of that grease.

00:03:36.858 --> 00:03:39.720
When the filters no
longer can hold the grease

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:43.617
and once that grease starts
getting heat and melting,

00:03:43.617 --> 00:03:45.092
you have the potential of that grease

00:03:45.092 --> 00:03:46.758
dropping into your food.

00:03:46.758 --> 00:03:48.780
And at that point, it's a health issue.

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.710
I've seen restaurants been shut down

00:03:50.710 --> 00:03:53.310
by the health department
for not cleaning their hood.

00:03:53.310 --> 00:03:55.348
I've seen restaurants been shut down

00:03:55.348 --> 00:03:59.486
by the fire marshal for not
maintaining their hoods.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:04.440
We take precaution in protecting the area

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:05.730
from contamination.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:08.237
We use two layers of plastic sheeting

00:04:08.237 --> 00:04:10.318
in order to protect the appliances

00:04:10.318 --> 00:04:12.930
from coming in contact with any chemical.

00:04:12.930 --> 00:04:15.265
The second layer of plastic sheeting

00:04:15.265 --> 00:04:17.431
is wrapped around the hood,

00:04:17.431 --> 00:04:19.714
and then a funnel is created.

00:04:19.714 --> 00:04:23.910
The funnel is used to drain
the chemical, grease, and water

00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:25.670
into the plastic containers.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:31.920
The plastic sheeting also
serves as a protective barrier

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:35.220
in preventing the chemical
from coming in contact

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:37.143
with the food-preparation area.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.880
Once we're completed and the
plastic sheeting is removed,

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.796
we'll conduct a freshwater wipe-down

00:04:44.796 --> 00:04:47.805
and recommend to the client
that they do the same

00:04:47.805 --> 00:04:50.223
prior to preparing any food.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.910
According to NFPA 96,

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:57.420
which is the agency that
regulates this industry,

00:04:57.420 --> 00:05:01.107
the frequency of cleanings
depends on the style of cooking

00:05:01.107 --> 00:05:03.840
you're conducting and
the frequency of cooking.

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:06.480
For example, if you
cook with solid fuel --

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:09.750
solid fuel is basically
cooking with charcoal --

00:05:09.750 --> 00:05:12.660
those are 30-day mandatory cleanings,

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.170
because they produce a lot more grease,

00:05:16.170 --> 00:05:19.770
and the embers that
come from the solid fuel

00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:21.540
can ignite the grease.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.300
If you're an occasional cooking facility,

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.365
those would be a year.

00:05:27.722 --> 00:05:29.825
Narrator: Maurice Patterson, who co-owns

00:05:29.825 --> 00:05:32.040
MMP Environmental Solutions,

00:05:32.040 --> 00:05:33.990
walks us through how
he cleans a grease trap

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:35.498
in a restaurant kitchen.

00:05:36.394 --> 00:05:39.030
Maurice: Today we will be
cleaning a commercial grease trap

00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:40.893
in a Mediterranean restaurant.

00:05:42.180 --> 00:05:43.500
The function of a grease trap

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:47.507
is to prevent any solids,
fats, oil, and greases

00:05:47.507 --> 00:05:50.119
from dishwasher or dishwashing sinks

00:05:50.119 --> 00:05:52.658
from entering your piping system.

00:05:52.658 --> 00:05:55.170
So, the cleaning process typically starts

00:05:55.170 --> 00:05:58.890
with me inspecting it,
making sure it's functional.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:01.233
Typically, you want to
clean your grease trap off

00:06:01.233 --> 00:06:02.490
every three months.

00:06:02.490 --> 00:06:04.290
I would say that it hasn't been serviced

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:06.540
most likely close to a year.

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:08.160
Then we unplug the grease trap,

00:06:08.160 --> 00:06:11.520
and we start to remove all the components.

00:06:11.520 --> 00:06:12.630
From that step,

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.788
we will prepare them to be
cleaned with a pressure washer.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.680
So, right here we're doing our pump-out.

00:06:22.680 --> 00:06:25.530
We have the wand connected to
the high-powered vacuum pump.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:29.010
We'll use the wand to pump
out the entire grease trap.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:30.368
So as you can see, there's a lot of grease

00:06:30.368 --> 00:06:31.810
sitting on top of the grease trap,

00:06:31.810 --> 00:06:34.676
and you can get a glimpse of solids

00:06:34.676 --> 00:06:37.320
that have floated to the top as well.

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:38.670
For Mediterranean restaurants,

00:06:38.670 --> 00:06:40.912
you're going to get a
lot of oils, cheeses.

00:06:40.912 --> 00:06:42.330
You're also going to get a lot of rice.

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:45.009
The rice is usually the main contents

00:06:45.009 --> 00:06:46.760
that sink to the bottom of the grease trap

00:06:46.760 --> 00:06:49.110
that you're going to want
to pump out regularly.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.850
I would describe it as yellow grease.

00:06:50.850 --> 00:06:53.643
So, yellow grease is
typically grease that's used

00:06:53.643 --> 00:06:55.863
to make fries or things like that.

00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:58.460
There's actually two
different types of grease

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:59.400
known in the industry.

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:00.625
You have brown grease.

00:07:00.625 --> 00:07:02.466
That's typically grease that's scraped off

00:07:02.466 --> 00:07:04.957
from pots and pans that's traveled down

00:07:04.957 --> 00:07:07.860
into your dishwasher and your
sink down to your grease trap.

00:07:07.860 --> 00:07:09.180
And then you also have yellow grease.

00:07:09.180 --> 00:07:11.250
You can actually sell it certain places.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:12.975
They may recycle that grease

00:07:12.975 --> 00:07:15.183
and turn it into biofuel,
things like that.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:17.610
It's considered liquid gold.

00:07:17.610 --> 00:07:19.717
Some companies, I'll
actually pay them for it,

00:07:19.717 --> 00:07:22.113
so they're incentivized to make sure

00:07:22.113 --> 00:07:24.900
that it's not going down the drain.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:26.460
Here, what I'm using is a putty knife.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:28.882
I'm scraping just old food waste

00:07:28.882 --> 00:07:30.675
that has collected on there.

00:07:32.670 --> 00:07:35.188
Organic waste just turns black over time,

00:07:35.188 --> 00:07:37.740
gets oxidized and turns black.

00:07:37.740 --> 00:07:40.860
It's a health risk, mainly
because any type of overflows

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.018
from your grease trap may
come in contact with food.

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:49.192
We use a pressure washer just to make sure

00:07:49.192 --> 00:07:52.792
we're getting all the
residual solids cleaned out.

00:07:56.490 --> 00:07:59.670
I'm using steel wool just
to do a final scrub-down,

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:02.252
make sure I'm getting into
all the nooks and crannies,

00:08:02.252 --> 00:08:03.660
all the tight spaces.

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:09.173
I do this right before
we do a final spray-down

00:08:09.173 --> 00:08:10.758
with the pressure washer.

00:08:20.820 --> 00:08:22.480
So, we dispose of the grease

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:24.163
at a water-pollution-control facility

00:08:24.163 --> 00:08:26.250
that will accept the
fats, oil, and grease.

00:08:26.250 --> 00:08:28.950
And there's not many
places that do accept it,

00:08:28.950 --> 00:08:30.480
so it's limited,

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:34.475
but we dispose of it in an
environmentally responsible way.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.650
When grease goes down the drain

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:42.510
with the dishwater, it's typically hot.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:46.017
So, grease or oils, when
they're hot, they're liquefied.

00:08:46.017 --> 00:08:48.777
When they cool down, they
solidify in the drain.

00:08:48.777 --> 00:08:49.950
That may cause plugging,

00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:53.130
which will in turn cause
backups, overflows.

00:08:53.130 --> 00:08:55.200
If it negatively impacts
their sewer system,

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.240
then they can incur fines.

00:08:57.240 --> 00:09:00.030
They will have to buy a new grease trap

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:04.139
or pay plumbing fees to
rectify the problems.

00:09:06.984 --> 00:09:09.510
Narrator: Steve, the
owner of Superior-Clean,

00:09:09.510 --> 00:09:13.403
walks us through how he cleans
a commercial exhaust system.

00:09:15.245 --> 00:09:16.620
Steve: We begin the cleaning process

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.350
by removing the kitchen hood filters

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:22.017
and wrapping the exhaust hood to ensure

00:09:22.017 --> 00:09:25.413
that we properly contain
all of the greasy water.

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:31.222
We'll start at the upper ductwork,

00:09:31.222 --> 00:09:33.720
where the grease accumulates the most.

00:09:33.720 --> 00:09:37.260
The top of the ductwork
here is where the grease,

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.050
the smoke, and the heat exits.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:42.660
The purpose of cleaning
the upper ductwork first

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:44.637
is to ensure that we properly flush

00:09:44.637 --> 00:09:47.910
the entire ductwork back
into the kitchen hood.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:50.183
So, our goal as a certified
hood-cleaning company

00:09:50.183 --> 00:09:52.225
is to ensure that all accessible areas

00:09:52.225 --> 00:09:54.630
of the hood and ductwork are bare metal,

00:09:54.630 --> 00:09:57.137
meaning there is no grease left over

00:09:57.137 --> 00:10:00.183
and there's no possibility
of a kitchen fire.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:04.500
So, the way we start degreasing the system

00:10:04.500 --> 00:10:06.383
is with a high-foaming agent,

00:10:06.383 --> 00:10:10.216
and we can mix in sodium
or potassium hydroxide

00:10:10.216 --> 00:10:13.590
to strengthen up our mix.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:16.707
From there, we will
scrape down the ductwork

00:10:16.707 --> 00:10:19.745
to ensure that all the
thick grease is removed

00:10:19.745 --> 00:10:21.636
before we come back and hit it

00:10:21.636 --> 00:10:24.903
with high-pressure steam cleaning.

00:10:24.903 --> 00:10:27.833
So, what you're seeing here in
this fast-food establishment

00:10:27.833 --> 00:10:29.730
is the fatty grease that are being pulled

00:10:29.730 --> 00:10:32.970
into the exhaust system,
and it's very watery.

00:10:32.970 --> 00:10:36.420
Much different than you'll
see in Chinese places

00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:39.840
or steakhouses that have
more of a carbonized grease.

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:43.003
Steakhouses are generally built-on carbon.

00:10:43.003 --> 00:10:47.612
Chinese places, you have
extremely thick, gooey grease.

00:10:47.612 --> 00:10:50.820
We typically see with wok, peanut oil,

00:10:50.820 --> 00:10:53.790
it becomes a very rubbery substance.

00:10:53.790 --> 00:10:56.250
It's almost like a
rubberized Gorilla Glue.

00:10:56.250 --> 00:10:58.050
It's extremely difficult to clean,

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:01.863
and it requires a lot
of scraping, degreasing.

00:11:03.330 --> 00:11:07.050
It moves down vertically
to a horizontal 90 bend,

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:10.650
and that's where the
inline exhaust fan sits.

00:11:10.650 --> 00:11:11.760
To the left of the system,

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.350
there are two access panels,

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:15.545
which gives you the
accessibility to scrape,

00:11:15.545 --> 00:11:18.078
degrease, and steam clean
inside of the ductwork

00:11:18.078 --> 00:11:20.675
to make sure that the
system remains bare metal,

00:11:20.675 --> 00:11:22.991
and that leads you into the kitchen,

00:11:22.991 --> 00:11:25.174
where the exhaust hood is.

00:11:25.174 --> 00:11:27.630
Exhaust fans are generally
the dirtiest part

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:29.528
of the kitchen exhaust system

00:11:29.528 --> 00:11:34.528
due to it being the last point
of passage before it exits.

00:11:34.590 --> 00:11:38.792
We just finished off with steam
cleaning the upper ductwork,

00:11:38.792 --> 00:11:40.050
which flushes the grease

00:11:40.050 --> 00:11:42.090
down through the inline exhaust fan,

00:11:42.090 --> 00:11:44.725
and now we're moving to the inner blades.

00:11:46.476 --> 00:11:49.050
So, what we're looking for
when applying the degreaser

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:53.430
on the exhaust fan blades
in the ductwork is the foam.

00:11:53.430 --> 00:11:56.638
Once you start to see
that the grease is turning

00:11:56.638 --> 00:11:59.797
into a yellowish tint of
a color and it's foaming,

00:11:59.797 --> 00:12:01.495
you know that you are able to hit it

00:12:01.495 --> 00:12:03.600
with the hot-water pressure washer.

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:05.550
The temperature of the
hot-water pressure washer

00:12:05.550 --> 00:12:08.430
allows the grease to really soften up,

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:09.720
and it moves a lot easier

00:12:09.720 --> 00:12:13.020
than trying to do with a
cold-water pressure washer.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.583
Now that we have flushed
the entire ductwork

00:12:15.583 --> 00:12:16.933
back into the kitchen,

00:12:16.933 --> 00:12:19.866
we are able to apply a low-pressure,

00:12:19.866 --> 00:12:22.350
high-steam rinse of the kitchen hood.

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:25.123
So, the opening to the
ductwork in the hood system

00:12:25.123 --> 00:12:27.930
starts behind the hood filters.

00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:31.710
The way we clean this is exactly
as the rest of the system.

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:34.508
We do not neglect any areas of the duct.

00:12:34.508 --> 00:12:39.107
This needs to be degreased,
scraped, and steam cleaned.

